  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>From the Old Port of Cannes, several shuttle boat companies reach the Lérins Islands archipelago in a few minutes: Sainte-Marguerite, wooded and wild, and Saint-Honorat, home to a Cistercian abbey.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Both shuttle companies say so on their websites: <strong>Planaria</strong> (to Saint-Honorat) states that "dogs are accepted provided they are kept on a leash", and <strong>Trans Côte d'Azur</strong> (to Sainte-Marguerite) writes that dogs are welcome on board and on the island, on a leash. The city of Cannes also reminds visitors on its Lérins Islands page to keep their dog on a leash and to stay on the marked paths. Island by island: <strong>Sainte-Marguerite</strong> (168 ha, 152 of them classified as a managed biological reserve since 2006, more than 130 bird species according to the ONF) tolerates leashed dogs on all beaches and coves, but camping, fires and smoking are prohibited outside paved areas and you must stay on the marked trails. <strong>Saint-Honorat</strong> (37 ha, Cistercian monastery of 21 monks) also allows leashed dogs along its 3 km of coastline, but the 8 ha vineyard is strictly off-limits (reserved for the monks), bicycles are forbidden, and silence is requested near the place of worship — whether dogs are admitted inside the abbey itself could not be confirmed. The companies' websites do not say whether the dog travels free or pays: ask the operator when booking (Planaria: 04 92 98 71 38).</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the islands have no waste-bag dispensers — bring your own supply to respect these protected natural sites.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Sainte-Marguerite offers long wooded walks, while Saint-Honorat suits a shorter visit around the abbey and its vineyard.</p>
